      cross_domain_range: [80, 0.7, "d", "A"],
      // A specific, sustained work routine documented for the Hanover geodesic survey (1820-1844): Gauss personally took charge of 'making measurements during the day and reducing them at night, using his extraordinary mental capacity for calculations,' and designed the heliotrope specifically to improve the survey's positional accuracy over long distances by reflecting sunlight through a system of mirrors and a small telescope (src_gauss_mactutor_gauss). A specific, sustained, hands-on measurement-and-calculation practice, not an inference from his general reputation for rigor. One independent source directly on point, kept below the 85+ band accordingly.
      detail_orientation: [76, 0.58, "d", "A"],
      // A genuine, sustained, named multi-year partnership, not a nominal one: Gauss recommended Wilhelm Weber's 1831 hire to Gottingen, then worked directly with him from 1833 to build one of the world's first practical electromagnetic telegraphs, linking Gauss's observatory to Weber's physics institute by wire; the two also co-founded the Gottingen Magnetischer Verein (Magnetic Union) to coordinate international geomagnetic measurement and jointly compiled an 'Atlas of Geomagnetism' (src_gauss_ethw_weber, src_gauss_maglab -- two independent institutional sources beyond MacTutor's own brief mention of the same telegraph). A specific, named, multi-year, multi-project collaboration with a consistent partner.
      collaboration: [68, 0.6, "d", "A"],
      // A specific, dated, directly-quoted 1832 incident with a real documented interpersonal cost, not an inference from reputation. On reading Janos Bolyai's newly published Appendix on non-Euclidean geometry, Gauss told a third party Bolyai was 'a genius of the first order,' but wrote directly to Bolyai's father Farkas -- his own long-time correspondent -- that 'to praise it would amount to praising myself,' since the results 'coincide[d] almost exactly with my own meditations' of the past thirty to thirty-five years, work Gauss had never published or left written proof of among his surviving papers (src_gauss_mactutor_bolyai, src_gauss_mactutor_gauss). The documented outcome: this 'greatly upset' Janos Bolyai, who took it as a severe blow. Scored dual_edged rather than a flattering clean priority claim: Gauss's underlying claim was plausible (consistent with the independently-documented decades-withheld-results pattern scored under discipline), but he chose to assert it privately, to the father rather than the son, in a way that cost a genuinely original young mathematician real recognition rather than crediting the independent achievement or settling the matter by publishing his own prior work. Documented from one directly-quoted, dated incident; kept at moderate score/confidence rather than an extreme rather than treating a single (if well-attested) incident as defining.
      competitiveness: [60, 0.52, "d", "D"],
      // A specific, documented instance of evaluating work strictly on its mathematical merit against the strong social convention of his era that women could not be serious mathematicians, not a general 'progressive' characterization. Before learning the identity of his correspondent 'M LeBlanc' (a pseudonym Sophie Germain adopted specifically because she 'feared being ignored because she was a woman'), Gauss wrote admiringly to a colleague that LeBlanc had 'made himself enthusiastically familiar with higher mathematics' and 'showed how deeply he penetrated' his Disquisitiones Arithmeticae. On learning, via her own letter after a French-military incident forced the reveal, that LeBlanc was a woman, Gauss's documented response was direct, specific admiration rather than dismissal or embarrassment: 'how to describe to you my admiration and astonishment at seeing my esteemed correspondent M LeBlanc metamorphose himself into this illustrious personage' (src_gauss_mactutor_germain). Scored from this specific, quoted reaction, not from the achievement of corresponding with Germain itself.
