      collaboration: [62, 0.55, "d", "A"],
      // A genuinely unflattering, honestly-documented instance rather than a flattering narrative. For the 1927 Paris flight Lindbergh deliberately rejected celestial navigation as impractical for a single pilot (per src_lb_connor_smithsonian, sextant work would have meant flying one-handed while doing 15-minute manual calculations on no sleep) and relied entirely on dead reckoning. In May 1928, flying the Spirit again between Havana and Florida, this approach failed badly: in his own words (quoted in src_lb_connor_smithsonian, from his memoir The Autobiography of Values), 'I had flown at almost a right angle to my proper heading and it...put me close to three hundred miles off route!' Rather than dismiss the incident, he petitioned the White House to have Navy Lt. Cmdr. Philip Weems assigned as his personal navigation tutor and began training in celestial line-of-position methods within months -- a specific, dated reversal of an explicit prior position in direct response to a documented failure, not a vague claim of open-mindedness.
      belief_updating: [74, 0.6, "d", "A"],
